WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:06.047
Here is a mutable list of strings, called ids.

2
00:00:06.047 --> 00:00:08.303
It holds one payment id, pay_1.

3
00:00:08.533 --> 00:00:10.453
String is a subtype of Any.

4
00:00:10.453 --> 00:00:12.088
Every String is an Any.

5
00:00:12.300 --> 00:00:16.157
So it looks harmless to use this list as a mutable list of Any.

6
00:00:16.157 --> 00:00:18.116
Suppose the compiler allowed it.

7
00:00:18.333 --> 00:00:20.781
Now two names point at the same list.

8
00:00:20.781 --> 00:00:22.037
Nothing was copied.

9
00:00:22.667 --> 00:00:25.770
Through the Any name, adding 42 is legal.

10
00:00:25.770 --> 00:00:28.419
42 is an Int, and an Int is an Any.

11
00:00:28.633 --> 00:00:31.326
Now code on the String side reads slot one.

12
00:00:31.326 --> 00:00:32.892
It was promised a String.

13
00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:37.624
The compiler put a hidden cast to String on that read.

14
00:00:37.624 --> 00:00:41.651
42 is not a String, so the cast throws a ClassCastException.

15
00:00:41.867 --> 00:00:44.093
The bad value went in through one name.

16
00:00:44.093 --> 00:00:48.088
The crash comes out of the other, later, on a line with no cast in it.

17
00:00:49.200 --> 00:00:53.815
Java has the same problem, with a List of String and a List of Object.

18
00:00:54.033 --> 00:00:57.411
Same list, same 42, same crash on the read.

19
00:00:58.333 --> 00:01:02.159
So both compilers refuse the line that makes the second name.

20
00:01:02.367 --> 00:01:04.972
javac says the types are incompatible.

21
00:01:04.972 --> 00:01:08.332
kotlinc says the initializer type does not match.

22
00:01:09.133 --> 00:01:11.209
This rule is called invariance.

23
00:01:11.433 --> 00:01:17.904
A mutable list of A is a subtype of a mutable list of B only when A and B are the same type.

24
00:01:18.133 --> 00:01:20.454
It does not matter that String is an Any.

25
00:01:20.454 --> 00:01:24.077
The arrow between the elements does not carry over to the lists.

26
00:01:25.700 --> 00:01:27.315
You meet this in real code.

27
00:01:27.315 --> 00:01:32.817
You have a mutable list of CardPayment, and a function that takes a mutable list of Payment.

28
00:01:33.033 --> 00:01:34.685
The call does not compile.

29
00:01:34.685 --> 00:01:36.145
Don't reach for a cast.

30
00:01:36.145 --> 00:01:38.749
Ask what the function does with the list.

31
00:01:38.967 --> 00:01:42.732
If it only reads, make it take a read-only List of Payment.

32
00:01:42.732 --> 00:01:43.944
That call compiles.

33
00:01:43.944 --> 00:01:46.624
The last section of this lesson shows why.

34
00:01:47.633 --> 00:01:50.034
If it adds, the compiler just caught a bug.

35
00:01:50.034 --> 00:01:53.663
It could have put a BankTransfer into your list of card payments.
